"""Original Day3 journals and business-plan parent; run after image inputs exist."""
from base import *
IRS=('IRS: nonprofit corporate status and federal tax obligations','https://www.irs.gov/charities-non-profits/federal-tax-obligations-of-nonprofit-corporations')
USDA=('USDA: plan a new farm operation','https://www.farmers.gov/your-business/beginning-farmers/business-plan')
add('real-estate-business-plan','Real Estate Business Plan: Pipeline, Capacity & Cash Example','Build a real estate business plan around a defined service, a reconciled sales pipeline, delivery hours, fee assumptions and closing cash. Includes a worked example.','Real estate professional reviewing property folders, a house model and a calendar',[
('Choose the real estate business you are actually planning',p('A real estate business plan must first distinguish a client-services practice from property investing, development and rental management. Their customers, cash commitments and risks differ. This guide models a fictional residential client-services practice, not buying houses with borrowed money. Use the separate <a href="/journal/short-term-rental-business-plan/">short-term rental plan</a> for an accommodation operation.')+p('Define the customer, area, service boundary, responsible license holder and reason a client would choose you. A useful statement might be a bounded relocation service for a particular local buyer segment, supported by actual interviews and service capacity. Do not describe an entire city as an undifferentiated target market or substitute a population count for evidence of buyer intent.')),
('Make the pipeline a closed-cohort bridge',table('Fictional closed inquiry cohort',['Stage reached','People / engagements','Interpretation'],[['Qualified inquiries','12','After duplicate removal and eligibility/scope checks'],['Signed engagements','8','Included within the 12'],['Reached contract stage','6','Included within the 8'],['Completed transactions','4','Included within the 6']])+p('These are cumulative stage counts for the same completed observation cohort. Do not add them to report 30 prospects. Inquiry-to-completion is 4/12 = 33.33%; signed-to-completion is 4/8 = 50%. Neither is a market benchmark. An open pipeline needs separate current-stage records because later outcomes are still unknown.')+p('Keep an engagement ID, agreed next action, stage-entry date, responsible person and reason for a lost or postponed case. A signed engagement is not a completed transaction or cash receipt. Use the <a href="/journal/crm-for-solopreneurs/">solo CRM workflow</a> for that distinction without promising that a planner automatically connects to listing or transaction systems.')),
('Reconcile the fee and delivery assumptions',table('Illustrative reporting-period economics',['Line','Calculation','Amount'],[['Gross practice fee per completed case','Fictional negotiated amount','$6,000'],['Gross completed-case fees','4 x $6,000','$24,000'],['Practice retained share','75% assumption after agreed allocation','$18,000'],['Case-specific delivery costs','4 x $500','-$2,000'],['Period operating costs','Entered total','-$6,000'],['Remainder before owner pay and tax','18,000 - 2,000 - 6,000','$10,000']])+p('The $6,000 fee and 75% allocation are hypothetical contract inputs, not customary rates, legal entitlements or suggested commissions. Record the actual compensation agreement, permitted payment route and expense allocation. This example starts with practice income and does not treat transaction escrow, earnest money or a property sale price as operating revenue.')+p('Of 140 available owner hours, reserve 40 for administration and prospecting. Four cases at 18 direct hours use 72 of the remaining 100 hours, leaving 28 hours before unexpected work. Six similar cases would require 108 direct hours and exceed that allowance by eight. Review the service estimate before increasing a sales target.')),
('Separate completed income from money collected',p('Assume the practice has earned $18,000 of retained fees but collected only three $4,500 allocations. Collections are $13,500 and the fourth $4,500 remains outstanding. With $12,000 opening operating cash, $8,000 of listed payments and a separate $4,000 owner withdrawal, closing operating cash is $13,500. The outstanding amount is not money available for a withdrawal.')+p('If only three cases complete in the period, retained fees become $13,500, case-specific costs $1,500 and the modeled remainder $6,000 after the same $6,000 period costs. A delayed close can also delay receipt into the next period. Keep a dated forecast and a downside scenario rather than assuming the annual average arrives evenly every month.')),
('Document permissions and a 30-day evidence test',p('Confirm licensing, supervision, advertising, client-money handling and other obligations with the responsible regulator for the actual location and service. For example, <a href="https://www.trec.texas.gov/become-licensed/sales-agent">Texas TREC</a> describes its sales-agent and broker relationship. That Texas example is not a rule for every jurisdiction. This business worksheet neither establishes permission to trade nor interprets an individual agreement.')+p('During a bounded 30-day test, record authorized inquiries, genuinely qualified cases, time spent, written scope and costs. Set a spending ceiling and review overdue follow-ups weekly. Keep personal and transaction records in appropriately controlled systems; publish only anonymous planning summaries. Compare alternatives using actual service coverage and response requirements, not invented competitor ratings.')+p('Write the wider narrative in the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">business-plan template</a>; connect receipts to the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">cash forecast example</a>. The <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">business-plan guide</a> explains the evidence-to-decision process. <strong>Does this plan guarantee closings?</strong> No. <strong>Can gross fees be treated as take-home pay?</strong> No: agreed allocations, delivery costs, overhead, tax and cash timing must be considered separately.'))
],[SBA,('TREC: become a sales agent','https://www.trec.texas.gov/become-licensed/sales-agent')],'/planners/small-business-planner-2026-2028/')
add('nonprofit-business-plan','Nonprofit Business Plan: Mission, Funding & Delivery Capacity','Plan a nonprofit program with measurable outputs, restricted and unrestricted cash, volunteer capacity and a realistic funding gap. Includes an original worked example.','Community coordinator reviewing budget papers in a neighborhood food packing workspace',[
('Connect the mission to a deliverable program',p('A nonprofit business plan explains who the organization serves, what changes it seeks, how work is delivered and how resources support that work. An inspiring mission is necessary context, but it is not a staffing schedule or funding plan. Define a small service that can be counted and evaluated before projecting a larger impact.')+p('This fictional community support program schedules household assistance appointments. Its output is a completed appointment; its intended outcome is improved access to relevant support, which needs separate evidence and follow-up. The example does not claim that every appointment produces a measurable life improvement. Record the measurement method, consent, limitations and responsibility for review.')),
('Cost the program and show its boundaries',table('Illustrative monthly program budget',['Use','Assumption','Amount'],[['Direct appointment resources','120 x $45','$5,400'],['Coordination','Entered monthly cost','$2,400'],['Shared support costs','Entered allocation','$1,200'],['Total listed program and support cost','5,400 + 2,400 + 1,200','$9,000'],['Cost per planned appointment','9,000 / 120','$75']])+p('The $75 is an allocated planning cost, not an efficiency ranking or a promise that service quality is adequate. It includes only the stated items. Add actual safeguarding, insurance, technology, access, training, facilities and other applicable costs when relevant. Volunteer time is a separate resource constraint here; the model does not assign an accounting value to donated services.')+p('Use a dated budget version and identify whether a cost is direct, shared, fixed or volume-sensitive. A grant budget may use different eligible-cost definitions. Do not silently substitute the internal allocation for the funder\'s terms or assume that every program cost can be paid from every grant.')),
('Keep restricted funds distinct from available operating cash',p('Opening unrestricted cash is $10,000. Receiving $4,000 of unrestricted donations and paying the $9,000 listed budget leaves $5,000. A further $6,000 grant received for a different restricted purpose raises total bank cash to $11,000, but the $6,000 remains reserved for that purpose in this simplified planning example. Total bank cash is not the unrestricted spending balance.')+table('Fictional cash availability bridge',['Record','Amount','Planning treatment'],[['Unrestricted closing cash','$5,000','Available only after other actual commitments are checked'],['Separate restricted grant cash','$6,000','Reserved for the specified purpose'],['Total cash in bank','$11,000','Sum, not a single free-to-spend fund'],['Uncollected campaign pledges','$3,000','Not included in cash receipts']])+p('This is a cash-control illustration, not a revenue-recognition or nonprofit accounting standard. Maintain fund and award records, actual restrictions, releases and reconciliations with qualified oversight. A promise to donate is not a bank receipt, and a grant application is not an award. Preserve the original terms and evidence behind any reclassification.')),
('Reconcile volunteer capacity before promising outputs',p('Eight volunteers each offer four hours per week for four weeks: 128 hours. The proposed 120 appointments need one volunteer hour each, with another 20 hours for intake and scheduling. Required time is 140 hours, a 12-hour gap. The plan cannot cure that gap by labeling every volunteer hour as available for appointments.')+p('Reducing the target to 100 appointments uses 120 hours including the same 20 coordination hours, leaving an eight-hour buffer. With $45 of direct resources per appointment, the revised listed budget is $8,100: $4,500 direct, $2,400 coordination and $1,200 support. Unrestricted closing cash becomes $5,900 under the unchanged receipt assumptions. Record the service-access consequence as well as the cash saving.')+p('Absence, training and supervision can reduce usable hours. Assign a backup owner, define the response to a cancellation and distinguish a booked slot from a completed service. Collect only information needed for the program and use appropriate permissions before sharing beneficiary stories or photographs.')),
('Build governance and funding review gates',p('The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/charities-non-profits/federal-tax-obligations-of-nonprofit-corporations">IRS explains</a> that nonprofit corporate status does not automatically provide federal income-tax exemption. Verify the actual formation, tax, fundraising, employment and service obligations with responsible official sources and qualified advisers. This program plan is not an exemption application or a determination of eligibility.')+p('Give each funding line an owner, evidence state, expected decision date, receipt date and restriction. Label unsubmitted, submitted, awarded and received separately. Review the cash gap before signing a delivery commitment, especially where a funder reimburses after costs have been incurred. Choose a fallback that changes the program openly rather than borrowing from a restricted balance without authority.')+p('Use the <a href="/tools/business-startup-checklist/">startup evidence checklist</a> for assigned preparation, the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">narrative template</a> for the plan and the <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">planning guide</a> for review structure. <strong>Is a positive bank balance enough?</strong> No; restrictions and outstanding commitments matter. <strong>Is a lower cost per appointment automatically better?</strong> No; access, quality and outcomes require their own evidence.'))
],[IRS,('National Council of Nonprofits: business planning','https://www.councilofnonprofits.org/running-nonprofit/administration-and-financial-management/business-planning-nonprofits')],'/planners/small-business-planner-2026-2028/')
add('farm-business-plan','Farm Business Plan: Yield, Seasonal Costs & Cash Timing','Create a farm business plan that connects usable yield, sales channels, labor, seasonal spending and cash receipts. Work through a small crop example and downside case.','Grower checking a clipboard beside seedling trays and harvested produce',[
('Define one enterprise and its production unit',p('A farm business plan needs a specific enterprise before a whole-farm forecast. A mixed vegetable plot, livestock enterprise and nursery use different production cycles, land, labor and sales units. This guide models one fictional 12-week crop enterprise. It does not recommend a crop, yield, growing method or regional price.')+p('List the usable area, production cycle, saleable unit, intended buyers and delivery channel. Keep input quantities and cost assumptions traceable to supplier quotes, local agronomic guidance and observed records. A hectare, harvested kilogram and packed customer box cannot be substituted for one another without an explicit conversion and loss allowance. The <a href="https://www.farmers.gov/your-business/beginning-farmers/business-plan">USDA planning resource</a> is a starting framework for the wider operation.')),
('Reconcile harvest, grading loss and sales',table('Illustrative enterprise volume bridge',['Stage','Units','Meaning'],[['Harvested units','1,000','Consistent saleable-unit basis before grading'],['Rejected during grading','100','Not available for sale'],['Saleable units','900','1,000 - 100'],['Units sold in the modeled cycle','810','90% of saleable units'],['Saleable inventory left','90','Not yet sold; condition must still be checked']])+p('At a fictional $8 selling price, 810 units produce $6,480 of sales. Grading loss is 10% of harvested units, while sell-through is 90% of saleable units. Those percentages have different denominators. Record unsold and spoiled product separately, and do not assume a biologically perishable unit remains usable simply because a spreadsheet carries it forward.')+p('The example uses one grade and channel. A real enterprise may need separate prices for retail, wholesale and lower-grade output, plus packaging and transport costs for each. Preserve the evidence behind discounts, rejected deliveries and returns. A quoted buyer price is not a purchase commitment.')),
('Include the cycle costs and owner labor separately',table('Fictional 12-week cycle economics',['Cost or result','Amount','Basis'],[['Seeds and growing inputs','$1,400','Entered cycle cost'],['Land / facility allocation','$600','Entered cycle allocation'],['Water and energy','$400','Entered cycle cost'],['Paid labor','$1,500','Entered total cost'],['Packing and delivery','$600','Entered cycle cost'],['Total listed costs','$4,500','Sum of five lines'],['Sales','$6,480','810 x $8'],['Remainder before owner time and omitted items','$1,980','6,480 - 4,500']])+p('If the owner contributes 100 hours and uses a notional $18 hourly allowance for evaluating the enterprise, subtract $1,800 to leave $180. The allowance is not an additional cash payment in this example. Depreciation, financing, tax, equipment replacement and other whole-farm costs are outside the listed boundary and must be added to a full plan.')+p('Dividing $4,500 by $8 gives 562.5, so 563 sold units recover the listed cycle spending before those omitted items. This assumes the spending is already committed and the sale price is net of the stated packing/delivery boundary. It is a limited planning threshold, not proof of agronomic or commercial viability.')),
('Model the months before and after harvest',p('Opening enterprise cash is $6,000. Paying all $4,500 before any buyer receipts leaves $1,500 at the low point. If $5,600 is collected in the cycle, closing cash is $7,100 while $880 of the $6,480 sales remains uncollected. The cash trough matters even though the full-cycle result is positive.')+p('A lower-yield case starts with 800 harvested units. Keeping the same 10% grading rejection and 90% saleable sell-through gives 720 saleable units, 648 sold units and $5,184 sales. If the same $4,500 is committed, the remainder is $684 before owner time and the omitted items. Do not reduce costs automatically with yield unless the actual cost behaves that way.')+p('Map seed purchases, seasonal labor, lease dates, harvest windows and buyer settlement dates on a dated forecast. A late receipt can require cash support even when product has been sold. Keep household withdrawals, other enterprises and external financing distinct so one activity does not appear to fund itself by accident.')),
('Set production, buyer and cash review gates',p('Before committing acreage or equipment, test the buyer and delivery assumptions, verify land and water access, identify labor availability and obtain appropriate local technical guidance. Confirm the food, environmental, employment, land-use and business requirements relevant to the actual operation with responsible authorities. This planning example provides no crop treatment or safety instructions.')+p('Record forecast versus actual planted, harvested, rejected, sold and paid quantities. Review the bottleneck before expanding: a larger planted area may overload harvest labor, cooling, packing or buyer capacity. Use the <a href="/journal/farmers-market-business-plan/">market-stall plan</a> for a selling event; it is distinct from this production-cycle plan.')+p('The <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">business-plan template</a> captures the wider narrative and the <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">cash forecast</a> helps date the commitments. <strong>Does revenue equal farm income available for living costs?</strong> No. <strong>Can this small example support a loan application unchanged?</strong> No; use the lender\'s actual information requirements and your own evidence.'))
],[USDA,SBA],'/planners/small-business-planner-2026-2028/')
add('short-term-rental-business-plan','Short-Term Rental Business Plan: Occupancy, Turnover & Cash','Plan a short-term rental with available-night occupancy, stay-level cleaning costs, fees, cash timing and a lower-booking scenario. Includes a reconciled property example.','Property host reviewing a booking calendar beside linens and keys in a guest apartment',[
('Define the property, operating rights and guest offer',p('A short-term rental business plan must identify the property, the right to operate it, the intended guest, delivery responsibilities and the cash needed between bookings. This example models one fictional accommodation unit. It is not a property-investment return forecast, an offer to buy real estate or evidence that a particular location permits short stays.')+p('Specify sleeping capacity, amenities, minimum stay, check-in method, maintenance owner and guest-support coverage. Validate demand with dated local evidence and comparable offers whose location and stay conditions actually match. A large nightly rate from a peak weekend is not a monthly average, and a platform listing does not establish the right to host.')),
('Use available nights as a clearly defined denominator',table('Fictional 30-day month',['Measure','Calculation','Result'],[['Calendar nights','Period length','30'],['Owner-use nights','Unavailable for guests','2'],['Maintenance nights','Unavailable for guests','3'],['Guest-available nights','30 - 2 - 3','25'],['Occupied nights','Six stays of three nights','18'],['Available-night occupancy','18 / 25','72%'],['Calendar-night occupancy','18 / 30','60%']])+p('Both occupancy figures are mathematically valid, but answer different questions. Label the denominator when comparing months. Removing nights from availability can raise the reported percentage without increasing revenue. Preserve the calendar and reason for each blocked night rather than editing the denominator to improve the dashboard.')+p('At a fictional room rate of $150, room revenue is $2,700 and room average daily rate is $2,700 / 18 = $150. Cleaning fees are separate. Do not add a cleaning charge to room revenue and still call the resulting amount room ADR. Taxes collected for another party and refundable deposits need separate treatment under the actual arrangement.')),
('Reconcile booking income with stay-level costs',table('Illustrative monthly operating bridge',['Line','Calculation','Amount'],[['Room revenue','18 x $150','$2,700'],['Cleaning charges to guests','6 x $40','$240'],['Total modeled booking income','2,700 + 240','$2,940'],['Cleaning payments','6 x $55','-$330'],['Platform/payment fees','Hypothetical 3% of $2,940','-$88.20'],['Occupied-night supplies/utilities','18 x $12','-$216'],['Fixed period costs','Entered total','-$1,800'],['Remainder before omitted items','2,940 - 330 - 88.20 - 216 - 1,800','$505.80']])+p('The 3% is an assumption, not a current platform quote. Confirm the actual fee base, refunds, cleaning agreement and settlement rules. The result excludes financing, depreciation, owner time, tax and other unlisted costs; it is not an investment yield or take-home income. Repair reserves should be a deliberate cash allocation rather than an unexplained percentage.')+p('Six turnovers at 2.5 hours require 15 cleaning hours. If only 12 hours are available on the relevant dates, the plan has a three-hour gap despite empty nights elsewhere in the month. Secure actual backup capacity or change the booking pattern. A monthly total does not prove that a same-day turnaround can be delivered.')),
('Test fewer nights and delayed collections',p('A lower-booking case has 12 occupied nights in four three-night stays. At the same assumptions, room revenue is $1,800 and guest cleaning charges $160. Fees are $58.80, cleaning $220, night-sensitive costs $144 and fixed costs $1,800. The modeled remainder is negative $262.80. Occupancy is 12/25 = 48%; do not reuse six cleaning charges when the number of stays changes.')+p('In the base case, suppose only $2,400 of the $2,940 has been received by the month-end cutoff. With $5,000 opening cash and all $2,434.20 listed costs paid, closing cash is $4,965.80. Another $540 is expected later under the example assumptions. Settlement timing can therefore reduce cash while the operating bridge remains positive.')+p('Record booking date, stay date, expected settlement, actual receipt, cancellation and refund separately. Use actual platform statements to reconcile gross amounts and deductions. Do not count a held refundable deposit as operating money or assume a future booking cannot be cancelled.')),
('Build a launch and service checklist for the actual location',p('Verify local hosting permission, lease or association conditions, insurance, registration, tax and service obligations with the responsible authorities and relevant agreements. The <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/tips-on-rental-real-estate-income-deductions-and-recordkeeping">IRS rental recordkeeping resource</a> is a federal reference; it does not decide local permission or the tax treatment of this simplified example.')+p('Before opening the calendar, test the access process, maintenance response, cleaning handoff and backup contact using fictional guest details. State what happens when a repair removes available nights. Keep a spending ceiling for furniture and supplies and compare the downside cash need with actual funding before committing.')+p('Use the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">business-plan template</a>, <a href="/tools/business-startup-checklist/">startup checklist</a> and <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">planning guide</a> for the wider work. <strong>Does high occupancy prove profitability?</strong> No: rate, stay length and costs matter. <strong>Can this substitute for a lending or tax model?</strong> No; use complete property-specific records and the recipient\'s requirements.'))
],[SBA,('IRS: rental recordkeeping','https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/tips-on-rental-real-estate-income-deductions-and-recordkeeping')],'/planners/small-business-planner-2026-2028/')
add('security-business-plan','Security Company Business Plan: Staffing, Coverage & Cash','Build a security company business plan around defined service scope, billable coverage, paid hours, relief staffing and payroll cash. Includes a fictional contract example.','Security operations coordinator reviewing shift cards at an unmarked dispatch desk',[
('Define a permitted service before pricing coverage',p('A security company business plan should define the actual service, jurisdiction, qualified staff, client responsibilities and operating boundary before discussing growth. This guide models a fictional, properly authorized unarmed site-coverage service. It provides business scheduling and cash examples, not operational security, use-of-force or weapons instructions.')+p('Name the client type, coverage window, required reporting, escalation contact and acceptance evidence. A contract for a staffed post is different from mobile visits, investigations, alarm services or personal protection. Do not reuse one hourly margin across services with different qualifications, travel, equipment and supervision requirements. Verify what the company and each worker may lawfully provide.')),
('Separate client coverage hours from paid labor hours',table('Illustrative four-week coverage model',['Measure','Calculation','Hours'],[['Client coverage per week','2 posts x 8 hours x 5 days','80'],['Client coverage in four weeks','80 x 4','320'],['Additional paid handoff/training time','Entered example allowance','8'],['Total paid hours','320 + 8','328']])+p('Billing 320 hours does not mean paying only 320 hours. Document the actual handoff, travel, training, supervision, absence and relief arrangements. Two people each available for 40 hours provide exactly 80 hours before any absence; there is no spare coverage. A roster must satisfy the actual location, worker qualifications, timing and applicable employment rules, not just a monthly total.')+p('Allocate named, appropriately qualified coverage to each shift and identify the substitute before promising the contract. The model does not determine lawful shift lengths, breaks, overtime, employment status or minimum pay. Use the actual requirements and qualified professional guidance where needed.')),
('Price the full service boundary',table('Fictional contract economics',['Line','Calculation','Amount'],[['Contract billing','320 x $32','$10,240'],['Paid staffing cost','328 x $22 fully loaded assumption','-$7,216'],['Supervision allocation','Entered cost','-$600'],['Equipment allocation','Entered cost','-$300'],['Other period overhead','Entered cost','-$1,200'],['Listed cost total','7,216 + 600 + 300 + 1,200','$9,316'],['Remainder before omitted items','10,240 - 9,316','$924']])+p('The $32 fee and $22 staffing cost are hypothetical, not market rates or wage advice. Fully loaded means that the example input is intended to include the staffing cost components you identify; it is not proof that every required component has been included. Add actual payroll burdens, insurance, travel and administration to the relevant line without counting them twice.')+p('If an absence requires eight incremental paid relief hours at the same $22 cost, the extra $176 reduces the modeled remainder to $748. This scenario explicitly assumes an additional cost; an actual substitution may have a different payment effect. Keep that difference visible rather than treating relief capacity as free.')),
('Fund payroll timing before accepting the work',p('The four-week staffing cost is $7,216, or $1,804 each modeled week. If all $9,316 of listed costs are paid before the first client receipt and opening cash is $9,000, the projected trough is negative $316. That is an unfunded gap, not permission to run a negative bank balance. Establish actual funding, change the commitment or revise agreed payment timing before starting.')+p('Receiving the full $10,240 later would produce a mathematical end balance of $9,924 from the original $9,000, but that later balance does not solve the earlier inability to make payments. A monthly profit calculation can conceal a weekly payroll problem. Build a dated forecast and allow a downside where the receipt arrives later than expected.')+p('Reconcile scheduled hours, confirmed coverage, approved timesheets, billed hours and collected invoices separately. A filled roster is not proof that work occurred, and a client invoice is not a cash receipt. Keep sensitive staff, incident and client information in appropriately controlled systems; the public plan needs only aggregated assumptions.')),
('Use licensing and contract evidence as release gates',p('The <a href="https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/private-security/private-security">Texas Department of Public Safety</a> regulates private security in Texas, including armed and unarmed roles. Use its official guidance for a Texas operation and the responsible regulator elsewhere. This plan neither grants a license nor establishes that a worker or company is eligible for a particular assignment.')+p('Before a contract starts, verify the permitted service, company and individual credentials, required training, insurance, written scope, staffing availability and payment terms. Assign a review owner and record expiry dates where relevant. Keep an honest stop condition: unresolved permissions, uncovered shifts or unfunded payroll must not be marked ready merely because the forecast shows revenue.')+p('Use the <a href="/tools/business-startup-checklist/">startup checklist</a> for evidence and owners, and the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">business-plan template</a> for the narrative. The <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">business-plan guide</a> links the wider planning process. <strong>Is the hourly spread profit?</strong> No. <strong>Can monthly availability prove every shift is covered?</strong> No; validate the dated roster and substitutes.'))
],[SBA,('Texas DPS: private security','https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/private-security/private-security')],'/planners/small-business-planner-2026-2028/')
add('business-plan-for-a-loan','Business Plan for a Loan: Funding Uses, Cash & Repayment','Prepare a lender-facing business plan with a reconciled funding request, evidence register, cash timing and repayment downside. Includes a fictional equipment-finance case.','Workshop owner comparing a supplier quote, cash forecast papers and a calculator',[
('Start with the lender\'s actual request',p('A business plan for a loan should explain what the money will fund, why the business needs it, how the operation produces cash and how repayment behaves under a weaker scenario. Ask the actual lender for its required documents, forecast periods, eligibility conditions and preferred format. A polished template is not a substitute for those requirements.')+p('This guide is a fictional management-planning example, not a lending recommendation, loan quote or approval assessment. It does not recommend a lender or apply a universal credit threshold. Keep the business narrative distinct from personal or confidential application records, and share sensitive documents only through the intended authorized channel.')),
('Reconcile uses of funds and funding sources',table('Illustrative equipment project',['Use of funds','Amount','Evidence to obtain'],[['Machine purchase','$30,000','Dated supplier quote and inclusions'],['Installation / setup','$6,000','Site and installation estimate'],['Working-cash allocation','$9,000','Dated receipts/payments forecast'],['Total uses','$45,000','30,000 + 6,000 + 9,000']])+table('Matching sources',['Source','Amount','Status'],[['Owner contribution','$15,000','Example assumption, verify availability'],['Requested borrowing','$30,000','Requested, not approved or received'],['Total sources','$45,000','15,000 + 30,000']])+p('The schedule balances, but a balanced table does not prove that financing exists. Keep requested, approved, documented and received states separate. State whether tax, delivery, fees, contingency and installation are included in each quote. If a $2,000 fee is deducted from the amount received, the usable cash and funding gap must be recalculated; do not leave the original sources unchanged.')+p('Explain what happens if the requested amount is reduced or the machine arrives late. A smaller purchase, delayed start or phased project may change capacity and revenue assumptions. Preserve the earlier version so the reviewer can trace why the funding request changed.')),
('Connect the repayment case to operating cash',p('Assume a hypothetical lender schedule calls for $600 per month in total debt payments. This is an entered example, not a rate calculation or current product offer. The real plan needs the complete quoted interest basis, term, fees, repayment dates, variable-rate exposure and any final or irregular payment.')+table('Original monthly cash sensitivity',['Measure','Base','Downside'],[['Cash after listed operating and tax payments, before debt and owner withdrawals','$1,100','$700'],['Entered debt payment','$600','$600'],['Simple cash-to-payment ratio','1.83','1.17'],['Planned owner withdrawal','$500','$500'],['Remaining monthly cash movement','$0','-$400']])+p('The ratio divides the first line by the debt payment. It is a narrow planning comparison, not a formally calculated lender debt-service-coverage ratio and not an approval cutoff. A lender may define available cash, debt, add-backs and periods differently. The owner withdrawal matters to the cash plan even though it is outside that simple ratio.')+p('If the downside lasts four months, the $400 monthly shortfall uses $1,600. An opening $9,000 working-cash allocation would fall to $7,400 before any other omitted movement. The forecast must still include actual tax dates, capital spending, seasonality and other debt. An average month does not establish the low point.')),
('Attach an evidence register, not unsupported certainty',table('Lender-facing evidence checklist',['Claim','Supporting record','Common mismatch'],[['Customer demand','Signed orders, verified history or clearly labeled research','Pipeline described as guaranteed sales'],['Delivery capacity','Hours, equipment availability and lead-time records','New equipment assumed productive immediately'],['Project cost','Current comparable quotes and scope','Installation or tax omitted'],['Opening position','Reconciled balances and commitments','Bank cash confused with unrestricted spare cash'],['Repayment timing','Actual proposed payment schedule','Annual totals used to hide a monthly gap'],['Downside response','Dated actions and funding limits','Emergency funding assumed without agreement']])+p('Separate established results from projections. Label customer concentration, supplier dependence, delayed collections and untested sales channels. If an assumption is not supported, name the evidence needed and the person responsible. Do not invent orders, endorsements, reviewer experience or funding commitments to strengthen the application.')+p('The <a href="https://www.sba.gov/funding-programs/loans">SBA loan information</a> explains its programs at a general level; eligibility and lender documentation still need current verification. This article does not reproduce a program\'s changing limits, rates or ownership rules.')),
('Assemble the plan in a reviewable order',p('Write the business and project summary, buyer evidence, delivery plan, management responsibilities, funding uses, financial assumptions and downside response. Cross-reference a document index so quotes, forecasts and historical records can be found without duplicating private files throughout the narrative. Check that the requested amount matches both the sources-and-uses schedule and the dated cash forecast.')+p('Use the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">business-plan worksheet</a> for an initial narrative, the <a href="/tools/business-plan-cover-page/">cover-page tool</a> for a dated front sheet and the <a href="/journal/business-plan-guide/">business-plan guide</a> for the wider process. These free resources do not replace lender forms, a full financial model or professional review.')+p('<strong>Will following this outline secure a loan?</strong> No. <strong>Should the plan hide a weak scenario?</strong> No; show the consequence and a realistic response. <strong>Is borrowed money operating revenue?</strong> No; keep financing sources separate from the trading forecast. Submit only accurate records you are authorized to share and review the actual agreement before committing.'))
],[SBA,('SBA: loan programs','https://www.sba.gov/funding-programs/loans')],'/planners/small-business-planner-2026-2028/')
add('bar-brewery-business-plan','Bar and Brewery Business Plan: Seats, Batches & Cash','Compare a bar and a brewery business plan using seat capacity, saleable batch volume, stock, costs and cash timing. Includes two distinct fictional operating models.','Taproom manager reviewing stock cards beside glassware and stainless brewing tanks',[
('Choose the operating model before building the forecast',p('A bar and brewery business plan must distinguish serving customers from producing stock. A bar may buy finished drinks and earn from seats, visits and average spend. A brewery also commits money and production capacity before saleable product is ready. A combined operation needs both models, with internal transfers and costs reconciled so revenue is not counted twice.')+p('This guide compares two fictional alternatives. It does not add their results into one forecast or provide production, alcohol-service or safety instructions. Define the actual premises, customer offer, permitted activities, responsible management and intended channels before using either model. Food, events, wholesale and accommodation require separate capacity and cost assumptions when included.')),
('Bar example: translate seats into visits and revenue',table('Illustrative bar month',['Measure','Assumption','Result'],[['Seats','Entered capacity','40'],['Potential turns per open day','Entered assumption','2'],['Open days','Entered calendar','24'],['Potential visits','40 x 2 x 24','1,920'],['Realized share of potential visits','65% assumption','1,248 visits'],['Average spend per visit','Fictional price/mix input','$22'],['Modeled revenue','1,248 x $22','$27,456']])+p('The 65% is a simplified utilization assumption, not an observed occupancy benchmark or a prediction. Visits are not unique customers, and seat turns are not a measure of drinks served. Validate the offer and staffing plan with evidence appropriate to the actual business. Split weekday and weekend periods when a monthly average hides peak constraints.')+p('At a hypothetical 32% variable purchase-and-consumption cost, the cost is $8,785.92. Subtract $8,500 of staffing, $5,000 of premises/utilities and $2,000 of other listed period costs to leave $3,170.08 before owner time, financing, tax and unlisted items. Verify the cost boundary: purchased stock is not automatically the amount consumed in the same month.')),
('Brewery example: reconcile produced, saleable and sold volume',table('Fictional separate production cycle',['Stage or amount','Result','Explanation'],[['Initial batch volume','1,000 liters','Common unit for the example'],['Process / packing loss','100 liters','Not available for sale'],['Saleable packed volume','900 liters','1,000 - 100'],['Volume sold','720 liters','80% of packed volume'],['Closing saleable stock','180 liters','Condition and storage still require checks'],['Total listed production cost','$2,200','Allocated across 900 saleable liters'],['Production cost assigned to sales','$1,760','720/900 x $2,200'],['Closing stock cost','$440','180/900 x $2,200']])+p('At an assumed net selling price of $6 per liter, sales are $4,320. After the $1,760 assigned production cost and $1,200 of listed cycle-period support costs, the simplified operating remainder is $1,360. The sales price is hypothetical and the model excludes excise and other taxes, financing, owner pay, depreciation and unlisted costs. It is not a complete accounting or investment forecast.')+p('If all production and support spending is paid and all sales collected, cash rises by $920: $4,320 - $2,200 - $1,200. The $440 difference from the operating remainder is tied up in closing stock. Unsold production is not cash, and a booked wholesale order is not necessarily a collected receipt.')),
('Capacity, storage and licensing are decision gates',p('List the actual production-cycle duration, equipment bottleneck, packaging capacity, storage, staff availability and channel lead time. One vessel does not provide unlimited monthly batches. A larger batch may consume cash and storage without solving demand. Use an explicit loss and stock-condition assumption and compare actual batch records to the forecast.')+p('The <a href="https://www.ttb.gov/business-central/requirements-brewery-operations">TTB brewery operations resource</a> describes federal requirements after qualification. A business must verify its actual federal, state and local permissions, tax and reporting obligations, premises conditions and service rules. A bar and a producing brewery can have different requirements. This article does not establish qualification or permission to produce or sell alcohol.')+p('Before signing a premises or equipment commitment, identify the responsible authority and the evidence required for the specific activity. Build time and funding for the actual review process; do not present an assumed approval date as guaranteed. Obtain qualified technical and legal guidance for operational and compliance matters.')),
('Write the plan and one downside before committing',p('Record the customer offer, operating model, route to permitted operation, customer research, channel plan, staffing, capacity, quotes, cash dates and review owner. Keep sales by channel and stock units consistent. A liter, package, visit and customer are different units and should not share one unlabeled revenue driver.')+p('For the bar example, reducing realized visits to 960 at the same $22 spend gives $21,120 revenue. With 32% variable costs and unchanged $15,500 period costs, the remainder is negative $1,138.40. That downside belongs in the plan before an equipment purchase or long premises commitment, not after the base case is presented as certain.')+p('Use the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">business-plan template</a> for the narrative and the <a href="/journal/business-plan-for-a-loan/">loan-plan guide</a> if a financing request is part of the project. <strong>Should bar and brewery revenue simply be added?</strong> No; model the actual arrangement and avoid counting internal transfers as a second external sale. <strong>Does a positive example prove demand or permission?</strong> No; both require independent evidence.'))
],[SBA,('TTB: requirements for brewery operations','https://www.ttb.gov/business-central/requirements-brewery-operations')],'/planners/small-business-planner-2026-2028/')
add('business-plan-guide','Business Plan Guide: From Evidence to a Working Cash and Capacity Plan','Write a useful business plan by connecting customer evidence, an operating model, capacity, cash, funding and review actions. Includes an original workflow and linked examples.','Overhead planning notebook, calculator and objects representing several small business models',[
('Decide the reader and the decision first',p('A business plan is a documented explanation of a business, the evidence behind its assumptions, the resources it needs and the decisions it will guide. Start by naming the reader and the decision. A founder deciding whether to run a small test needs a different level of detail from a lender reviewing a funding request or a board committing restricted program resources.')+p('The <a href="https://www.sba.gov/business-guide/plan-your-business/write-your-business-plan">SBA distinguishes lean and traditional formats</a>. Choose a format that serves the reader rather than treating page count as quality. A short operating plan may be useful internally; a recipient may require statements, projections, ownership information and supporting records beyond it. Ask for those requirements before drafting.')+p('This guide owns the planning process. The <a href="/journal/business-plan-examples/">business-plan examples directory</a> helps choose an operating model, the <a href="/tools/business-plan-template/">editable template</a> captures a narrative, and the <a href="/tools/business-startup-checklist/">startup checklist</a> tracks evidence and actions. They are different jobs, not interchangeable versions of the same page.')),
('Turn the customer problem into a testable offer',table('Original evidence-to-offer workflow',['Step','Record','Decision'],[['Customer and problem','Who experiences what problem, when and how often','Is the target narrow enough to test?'],['Alternatives','Current workarounds, competing offers and doing nothing','Why would this buyer change?'],['Offer boundary','Deliverable, selling unit, price basis and exclusions','Can the promise be explained and delivered?'],['Evidence','Dated observation, source and limitation','What is known versus assumed?'],['Next test','Owner, time/spend ceiling and stop rule','What result changes the plan?']])+p('Do not describe interviews as orders or a broad search-volume figure as proven customer demand. Keep exact observations separate from your interpretation. Five useful conversations can reveal objections but do not establish a representative conversion rate. Competitor pages can show how an offer is framed; they do not reveal the competitor\'s actual profit or customer satisfaction.')+p('Before taking payments or making commitments, confirm the requirements relevant to the actual activity and location. The plan should have an evidence owner and unresolved questions. It should not certify legal compliance, tax status or a regulated qualification merely because a checklist row was marked done.')),
('Choose an operating model and reconcile its units',table('Pick the constraint that drives the plan',['Model','Primary unit and bottleneck','Useful worked guide'],[['Client services','Accepted projects and delivery hours','<a href="/journal/accounting-firm-business-plan/">Accounting practice</a>'],['Stocked products','Units purchased, sold, returned and still usable','<a href="/journal/ecommerce-business-plan/">Ecommerce</a>'],['Seasonal production','Harvest, grading, saleable yield and dated spending','<a href="/journal/farm-business-plan/">Farm enterprise</a>'],['Property hosting','Available nights, stays and turnover dates','<a href="/journal/short-term-rental-business-plan/">Short-term rental</a>'],['Staffed coverage','Billable post-hours and qualified paid coverage','<a href="/journal/security-business-plan/">Security company</a>'],['Mission delivery','Outputs, volunteer capacity and fund restrictions','<a href="/journal/nonprofit-business-plan/">Nonprofit program</a>'],['Production plus venue','Saleable batches, stock and customer visits','<a href="/journal/bar-brewery-business-plan/">Bar and brewery</a>']])+p('A business can combine models, but each driver needs a consistent unit and cost boundary. Reconcile opening stock plus receipts less dispatches plus usable returns to closing stock. For service work, compare actual hours with available delivery time after administration and buffer. A page of revenue goals does not resolve either constraint.')+p('The new <a href="/tools/business-idea-comparison/">business idea comparison</a> helps compare up to five candidates using your entered weights and ratings. Its score is subjective decision support, not a market forecast. Use it before choosing a model; do not reverse-engineer scores to make an already preferred idea win.')),
('Worked service plan: connect price, hours and cash',p('Consider a fictional $300 service with $60 of direct cost and a six-hour delivery estimate. Four projects produce $1,200 revenue and $240 direct cost, leaving $960 before overhead, owner time and tax. If $400 of listed period overhead is allocated, the modeled remainder is $560. None of these amounts is a market benchmark.')+p('The owner has 40 hours in the period, with 10 reserved for administration. Four projects need 24 of the 30 remaining hours, leaving six hours before unexpected work. If each actually takes eight hours, four need 32 hours and exceed the allowance by two. Reduce bookings, change scope or establish additional capacity rather than leaving the delivery promise unchanged.')+p('At 50% deposits, the four projects initially collect $600, not the full $1,200. If all $240 direct cost and $400 overhead are paid before final balances arrive, cash falls by $40 despite a positive operating remainder. Opening cash of $1,000 becomes $960 at that point. The other $600 is expected later, assuming all work and payment conditions are met.')+p('Put these dates in a forecast. The <a href="/journal/cash-flow-forecast-template-example/">cash-flow guide</a> explains the bridge, while the <a href="/tools/daily-schedule-builder/">daily schedule builder</a> checks whether today\'s commitments overlap. A calendar tool does not establish customer demand or reconcile the whole business ledger.')),
('Document funding and a downside before the narrative polish',p('A funding section needs uses, sources, status, timing and evidence. Separate owner contributions and borrowing from operating sales. A request is not an approval, and an approval is not a cash receipt. Link each substantial use to a quote or another dated basis, then check what changes when timing or available funding differs.')+p('For the service example, losing one booking changes sales and direct cost, but may leave the $400 overhead unchanged. Three projects produce $900 sales, $180 direct cost and a $320 remainder before the same omissions. A delayed receipt changes cash timing without necessarily changing earned revenue. Change one assumption at a time when you want to understand the cause.')+p('Use the <a href="/journal/business-plan-for-a-loan/">business plan for a loan</a> guide for a lender-facing case and the <a href="/journal/real-estate-business-plan/">real-estate guide</a> for a closing-dependent client service. Neither supplies a universal approval threshold, legal entitlement or promised income.')),
('Assemble, review and preserve the next decision',table('Working plan acceptance checklist',['Part','Minimum useful evidence'],[['Summary','Customer, offer, model and decision requested'],['Demand','Sources, dates, actual observations and limits'],['Delivery','Owner, capacity, dependencies and acceptance criteria'],['Economics','Units, price, cost boundary and arithmetic'],['Cash','Opening balance, dated receipts/payments and low point'],['Risk','Downside, response, spending/time ceiling and stop condition'],['Review','Version, owner, next date and unresolved evidence']])+p('Write the executive summary after the workings are coherent. Keep supporting records in an organized index and avoid pasting sensitive customer, employee or applicant information into a public plan. Reconcile all repeated totals so the narrative, funding table and forecast do not tell different stories.')+p('Choose a fixed review date and compare forecast with actual evidence. Preserve the original version, explain changed assumptions and record a continue, revise or stop decision. Use the <a href="/tools/business-plan-cover-page/">cover-page builder</a> for document identification. A professionally presented cover does not replace evidence inside the plan.')+p('<strong>How long should a business plan be?</strong> Long enough for its reader and decision, with additional documents when required. <strong>Can I copy a worked example?</strong> Use its method, but replace every fictional assumption with your own evidence. <strong>When is a plan finished?</strong> A dated version can be ready for review while the business continues to learn; unresolved material assumptions should stay visible rather than being labeled verified.'))
],[SBA],'/planners/small-business-planner-2026-2028/')
def write():
 t=(R/'journal/business-planner-weekly-review/index.html').read_text();styles=t[t.index('<style>'):t.index('</style>')+8];nav=t[t.index('<header class="nav">'):t.index('</header>')+9];footer=t[t.index('<footer class="site">'):]
 image_inputs=json.loads((D/'image-inputs.json').read_text());manifest=[]
 for a in A:
  slug=a['slug'];src=Path(image_inputs[slug]);asset='/images/journal/'+slug+'-20261106.webp';dest=R/asset.lstrip('/');dest.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True,parents=True)
  with Image.open(src) as im:
   stream=BytesIO();im.resize((1200,800)).save(stream,'WEBP',quality=83,method=6);data=stream.getvalue();assert len(data)>1000;dest.write_bytes(data)
  url='https://nexalyplanner.com/journal/'+slug+'/'
  schema={'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'BlogPosting','headline':a['title'],'description':a['desc'],'image':'https://nexalyplanner.com'+asset,'mainEntityOfPage':url,'author':{'@type':'Organization','name':'NexalyPlanner','url':'https://nexalyplanner.com/about/'},'publisher':{'@type':'Organization','name':'NexalyPlanner'},'datePublished':'2026-11-06','dateModified':'2026-11-06','inLanguage':'en-US'}
  head=f'<!doctype html><html lang="en-US"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{E(a["title"])}</title><meta name="description" content="{E(a["desc"])}"><link rel="canonical" href="{url}"><meta property="og:image" content="https://nexalyplanner.com{asset}"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://nexalyplanner.com{asset}"><meta property="og:image:alt" content="{E(a["alt"])}"><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta property="article:published_time" content="2026-11-06"><meta property="article:modified_time" content="2026-11-06">{styles}<style>.reading h2{{scroll-margin-top:110px}}</style><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/style.css?v=7e6083e6f963"><script type="application/ld+json">{json.dumps(schema)}</script></head><body>'
  page=head+nav+f'<header class="article-hero"><div class="brand">NEXALYPLANNER JOURNAL</div><h1>{E(a["title"])}</h1><p class="metadata">By NexalyPlanner · Published November 6, 2026</p></header><main><p class="toplink"><a href="/journal/">Back to the Journal</a></p><article class="reading"><figure class="article-feature"><img src="{asset}" width="1200" height="800" alt="{E(a["alt"])}" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><figcaption>AI-generated editorial illustration matched to this topic. Screens and cards are conceptual; the worked HTML tables contain the exact example figures.</figcaption></figure>'+a['body']+f'<aside class="related-guides"><h2>Continue planning</h2><p><a href="{a["related"]}">View the relevant planner and its documented features</a></p><p><a href="/journal/business-plan-examples/">Compare business plan examples</a></p></aside></article></main>'+footer
  path=R/'journal'/slug/'index.html';path.parent.mkdir(exist_ok=True);path.write_text(page)
  manifest.append({k:a[k] for k in ['slug','title','alt','sources']}|{'asset':asset,'word_count':len(re.sub('<[^>]+>',' ',a['body']).split())})
 (D/'articles.json').write_text(json.dumps(manifest,indent=2));print([(a['slug'],a['word_count']) for a in manifest])
if __name__=='__main__':write()
